lessons.theory:
Numbers in JavaScript do not need quotes. `42` is a number, `"42"` is a string.

The arithmetic operators are `+`, `-`, `*`, `/` and `**` (power). Division always returns a decimal: `10 / 4` is `2.5`.

```javascript
let price = 9.99;
let quantity = 3;
let total = price * quantity;
console.log(total); // 29.97
```

JavaScript has only one number type, so there is no difference between integers and floats. Both are just `number`.

lessons.solution:
let width = 8;
let height = 5;
let area = width * height;

lessons.theory:
A **template literal** is a string between backticks. Inside it, `${expression}` is replaced by the value of that expression:

```javascript
let name = "Ana";
let score = 98;
console.log(`${name} scored ${score} points`);
// Ana scored 98 points
```

This is the modern replacement for string concatenation with `+`. You can put any JavaScript expression inside `${}`, not just variable names.

lessons.solution:
let city = "Madrid";
let temp = 22;
console.log(`${city} is ${temp} degrees`);

let x = 10;
let isPositive = x > 0;
let isTen = x === 10;

lessons.theory:
`&&` (AND) is `true` only when both sides are `true`.
`||` (OR) is `true` when at least one side is `true`.
`!` (NOT) flips a boolean.

```javascript
let age = 20;
let hasTicket = true;
let canEnter = age >= 18 && hasTicket;
console.log(canEnter); // true
```

JavaScript evaluates left to right and **short-circuits**: with `&&`, if the left side is `false`, the right side is never evaluated. With `||`, if the left side is `true`, the right side is skipped.

lessons.solution:
let hour = 14;
let isWeekday = true;
let officeOpen = hour >= 9 && hour <= 18 && isWeekday;

let points = 100;
points -= 20;
points *= 2;

lessons.theory:
An `if` statement runs its block only when the condition is `true`. `else` runs when it is `false`:

```javascript
let temperature = 15;
if (temperature < 0) {
  console.log("freezing");
} else {
  console.log("not freezing");
}
```

The curly braces `{}` define the block. Always include them, even for a single line. Omitting them causes subtle bugs when you later add a second line and forget the braces.

The condition must be inside parentheses: `if (condition)`.

lessons.solution:
let battery = 15;
if (battery < 20) {
  console.log("low battery");
} else {
  console.log("battery ok");
}

lessons.theory:
`else if` adds more branches between the initial `if` and the final `else`. JavaScript checks them in order and runs the first one whose condition is `true`:

```javascript
let grade = 72;
if (grade >= 90) {
  console.log("A");
} else if (grade >= 80) {
  console.log("B");
} else if (grade >= 70) {
  console.log("C");
} else {
  console.log("F");
}
```

Once a branch runs, the rest are skipped. Order matters: always put the most specific condition first.

lessons.solution:
let speed = 85;
if (speed > 120) {
  console.log("too fast");
} else if (speed > 90) {
  console.log("fast");
} else {
  console.log("normal");
}

lessons.theory:
`switch` compares one expression against several fixed values:

```javascript
let day = "Monday";
switch (day) {
  case "Saturday":
  case "Sunday":
    console.log("weekend");
    break;
  default:
    console.log("weekday");
}
```

Each `case` is followed by its value and a colon. The `break` statement exits the switch after a match. Without `break`, execution **falls through** into the next case, which is almost always a bug. `default` runs when no case matches.

lessons.solution:
let color = "red";
switch (color) {
  case "red":
    console.log("stop");
    break;
  case "green":
    console.log("go");
    break;
  default:
    console.log("caution");
}

lessons.theory:
The **nullish coalescing** operator `??` returns the right-hand side only if the left side is `null` or `undefined`. It ignores other falsy values like `0` or `""`:

```javascript
let username = null;
let display = username ?? "Guest";
console.log(display); // "Guest"

let count = 0;
let shown = count ?? 10;
console.log(shown); // 0  (because 0 is not null/undefined)
```

Compare this with `||`, which uses the fallback for any falsy value. When `0` or `""` are valid values, `??` is the correct tool.

A `for` loop has three parts in its header: initialize, condition, update:

```javascript
for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  console.log(i);
}
// prints 0 1 2 3 4
```

- `let i = 0` runs once before the loop starts.
- `i < 5` is checked before every iteration. The loop stops when it is `false`.
- `i++` runs after every iteration.

The loop variable `i` exists only inside the loop block.

lessons.solution:
for (let i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
  console.log(i);
}

lessons.theory:
A `while` loop runs as long as its condition is `true`:

```javascript
let count = 3;
while (count > 0) {
  console.log(count);
  count--;
}
// 3, 2, 1
```

You are responsible for updating the variable inside the loop. Forget the update and you have an infinite loop that freezes the browser.

Use `while` when the stopping condition depends on something that changes inside the loop, like reading user input or draining a queue.

lessons.solution:
let n = 1;
while (n < 100) {
  console.log(n);
  n *= 2;
}

lessons.theory:
`for...of` loops over the **values** of any iterable, including arrays:

```javascript
const fruits = ["apple", "banana", "cherry"];
for (const fruit of fruits) {
  console.log(fruit);
}
```

This is simpler than a counter-based `for` loop when you just need every element. Use `const` for the loop variable because you are not reassigning it, only receiving each value.

Do not use `for...of` when you need the index; use `for` with a counter or `forEach` with a callback in that case.

lessons.solution:
const colors = ["red", "green", "blue"];
for (const color of colors) {
  console.log(color);
}

lessons.theory:
`break` immediately exits the loop, skipping the rest of that iteration and all future ones:

```javascript
for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
  if (i === 5) break;
  console.log(i); // 0 1 2 3 4
}
```

`continue` skips the rest of the current iteration and jumps to the next one:

```javascript
for (let i = 0; i < 6; i++) {
  if (i === 3) continue;
  console.log(i); // 0 1 2 4 5
}
```

Both work in `while` loops too.

lessons.solution:
for (let i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
  if (i % 2 === 0) continue;
  console.log(i);
  if (i === 7) break;
}

function square(n) {
  console.log(n * n);
}
square(7);

lessons.theory:
A function can **return** a value to the caller with the `return` keyword:

```javascript
function add(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}
let sum = add(3, 4);
console.log(sum); // 7
```

`return` immediately stops the function and hands the value back. Code after `return` in the same block never runs.

A function without a `return` statement (or with a bare `return`) gives back `undefined`. That is fine when the function's job is a side effect like logging.

lessons.solution:
function multiply(a, b) {
  return a * b;
}
let result = multiply(6, 7);
console.log(result);

lessons.theory:
Functions can be stored in variables. The modern syntax is the **arrow function**:

```javascript
const double = (n) => n * 2;
console.log(double(5)); // 10
```

When the body is a single expression, the `{}` and `return` are implied. For multi-line bodies you need both:

```javascript
const clamp = (value, min, max) => {
  if (value < min) return min;
  if (value > max) return max;
  return value;
};
```

Arrow functions are not hoisted. You must declare them before calling them.

You can specify a **default value** for a parameter by adding `= value` in the parameter list:

```javascript
function greet(name = "stranger") {
  return `Hello, ${name}!`;
}
console.log(greet("Ana"));  // Hello, Ana!
console.log(greet());       // Hello, stranger!
```

The default is used only when the argument is `undefined`, either because it was not passed or because `undefined` was passed explicitly.

lessons.solution:
function power(base, exp = 2) {
  return base ** exp;
}
console.log(power(3));
console.log(power(3, 3));

lessons.theory:
`let` and `const` are **block-scoped**: they exist only inside the `{}` where they were declared and in any nested blocks inside that.

```javascript
function test() {
  let x = 10;
  if (true) {
    let y = 20;  // only visible here
    console.log(x + y); // 30
  }
  console.log(x); // 10
  // console.log(y); // ReferenceError: y is not defined
}
```

Variables declared outside all functions are **global** and visible everywhere, which is usually a bad idea. Keep variables as close to where they are used as possible.

lessons.solution:
const planets = ["Mercury", "Venus", "Earth", "Mars"];
console.log(planets[0]);
console.log(planets.length);

const queue = ["a", "b", "c"];
queue.push("d");
queue.shift();
console.log(queue);

const temps = [0, 20, 37, 100];
const fahrenheit = temps.map(c => (c * 9/5) + 32);
console.log(fahrenheit);

lessons.theory:
`filter` calls its callback for each element and keeps only those for which the callback returns `true`:

```javascript
const ages = [12, 25, 16, 30, 14];
const adults = ages.filter(age => age >= 18);
console.log(adults); // [25, 30]
```

Like `map`, `filter` returns a new array and does not change the original. You can chain `filter` and `map` together:

```javascript
const result = ages.filter(a => a >= 18).map(a => a * 2);
```

lessons.solution:
const words = ["cat", "elephant", "ox", "rhinoceros", "dog"];
const longWords = words.filter(w => w.length > 4);
console.log(longWords);

const prices = [9.99, 4.50, 12.00, 7.25];
const total = prices.reduce((acc, p) => acc + p, 0);
console.log(total);

const book = {
  title: "Dune",
  author: "Frank Herbert",
  pages: 896
};
console.log(book.title);
console.log(book.pages);

lessons.theory:
You can add or overwrite properties on an object at any time using dot notation:

```javascript
const car = { brand: "Toyota", year: 2018 };
car.color = "blue";   // add new property
car.year = 2020;       // update existing property
console.log(car);
// { brand: "Toyota", year: 2020, color: "blue" }
```

Even though `car` is `const`, you can change its properties. `const` only prevents reassigning `car` itself to a different value.

lessons.solution:
const person = { name: "Leo", age: 25 };
person.age = 26;
person.city = "Barcelona";
console.log(person);

const product = { id: 42, name: "Keyboard", price: 79.99 };
const { name, price } = product;
console.log(name);
console.log(price);

const rgb = [255, 128, 0];
const [red, green, blue] = rgb;
console.log(red);

lessons.theory:
A property whose value is a function is called a **method**:

```javascript
const counter = {
  count: 0,
  increment() {
    this.count += 1;
  },
  value() {
    return this.count;
  }
};
counter.increment();
console.log(counter.value()); // 1
```

`this` inside a method refers to the object the method belongs to. This is how the method reads and writes the object's own properties.

In arrow functions `this` is NOT the object — use regular function syntax for methods.

lessons.solution:
const circle = {
  radius: 5,
  area() {
    return Math.PI * this.radius ** 2;
  }
};
console.log(circle.area());

lessons.theory:
`document.querySelectorAll(selector)` returns a **NodeList** of all matching elements:

```javascript
const items = document.querySelectorAll(".item");
items.forEach(item => {
  console.log(item.textContent);
});
```

A NodeList looks like an array and you can use `forEach` on it, but it is not an actual array. Convert it with `Array.from(items)` if you need `map`, `filter` or `reduce`.

`getElementById("id")` is an older, faster alternative when you only need to select by id.

Setting `textContent` replaces everything inside the element with plain text. Any HTML you write in the string is shown as literal characters, not rendered:

```javascript
const h1 = document.querySelector("h1");
h1.textContent = "New heading";
```

Setting `innerHTML` replaces the content with HTML that the browser parses and renders:

```javascript
const box = document.querySelector("#box");
box.innerHTML = "<strong>Bold text</strong>";
```

Only use `innerHTML` with strings you fully control. Never paste user input into `innerHTML`.

Every element has a `.style` object with one property per CSS rule, written in camelCase:

```javascript
const box = document.querySelector("#box");
box.style.color = "red";
box.style.fontSize = "24px";
box.style.backgroundColor = "#f0f0f0";
```

CSS property `background-color` becomes `backgroundColor` in JavaScript. Hyphenated names are always converted to camelCase.

Inline styles override stylesheet rules. For anything beyond a quick toggle, adding or removing CSS classes is a cleaner approach.

lessons.solution:
const alert = document.querySelector("#alert");
alert.style.backgroundColor = "yellow";
alert.style.fontSize = "18px";

const nav = document.querySelector("#nav");
nav.classList.add("visible");
nav.classList.toggle("open");

lessons.theory:
`document.createElement(tagName)` creates a new element that is not yet on the page. You configure it, then attach it:

```javascript
const li = document.createElement("li");
li.textContent = "New item";
document.querySelector("ul").appendChild(li);
```

`appendChild` adds the element as the last child of the target. `prepend` adds it first. `insertBefore` places it before a specific sibling.

Creating elements this way is safer than `innerHTML` because the browser never parses them as HTML.

lessons.solution:
const p = document.createElement("p");
p.textContent = "Hello from JS";
document.querySelector("#container").appendChild(p);

lessons.theory:
`addEventListener(eventName, callback)` tells an element to call a function when a specific event happens:

```javascript
const btn = document.querySelector("#btn");
btn.addEventListener("click", () => {
  console.log("clicked!");
});
```

The callback is called an **event handler** or **listener**. It runs every time the event fires.

Common event names: `"click"`, `"input"`, `"change"`, `"submit"`, `"keydown"`, `"mouseover"`, `"load"`.

You can add multiple listeners to the same element for the same or different events.

lessons.solution:
const myBtn = document.querySelector("#myBtn");
myBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
  console.log("Button clicked");
});

lessons.theory:
Every event handler receives an **event object** as its first argument. It holds details about what happened:

```javascript
document.addEventListener("keydown", (event) => {
  console.log(event.key);    // "Enter", "a", "ArrowUp" …
  console.log(event.type);   // "keydown"
});

btn.addEventListener("click", (e) => {
  console.log(e.target);     // the element that was clicked
  console.log(e.clientX);    // horizontal position of the mouse
});
```

`event.target` is particularly useful: it is the exact element that the user interacted with, even inside a complex tree.

lessons.solution:
document.querySelector("#box").addEventListener("click", (e) => {
  console.log(e.target);
  console.log(e.type);
});

lessons.theory:
Some events have a **default action** built into the browser. A click on an `<a>` navigates to its `href`. Submitting a form reloads the page. You can prevent these with `event.preventDefault()`:

```javascript
const form = document.querySelector("form");
form.addEventListener("submit", (e) => {
  e.preventDefault();          // stops the page reload
  console.log("handling form in JS");
});
```

This lets you handle the event entirely in JavaScript. You then decide what happens next.

lessons.solution:
document.querySelector("form").addEventListener("submit", (e) => {
  e.preventDefault();
  console.log("form submitted");
});

lessons.theory:
Events **bubble** up the DOM tree: a click on a child fires the click event on the child, then on its parent, then on its parent, and so on up to `document`.

**Event delegation** takes advantage of this: instead of adding a listener to each child, you add one listener to a parent and check `event.target`:

```javascript
const list = document.querySelector("ul");
list.addEventListener("click", (e) => {
  if (e.target.tagName === "LI") {
    console.log("clicked:", e.target.textContent);
  }
});
```

This works even for elements added after the listener was set up.

A **Promise** is an object that represents a value not yet available. It is in one of three states:

- **pending**: the operation is still running
- **fulfilled**: the operation succeeded and a value is ready
- **rejected**: the operation failed and an error reason is available

Once settled (fulfilled or rejected), a Promise's state never changes.

```javascript
const p = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  // async work here
  resolve("done");   // success
  // reject("error"); // failure
});
```

You almost never create Promises manually. You use them because `fetch` and other browser APIs return them.

`fetch` only rejects on network failure (no internet, DNS error). A 404 or 500 response is still a **fulfilled** Promise with `response.ok === false`.

Always check `response.ok` before parsing:

```javascript
fetch(url)
  .then(response => {
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error(`HTTP error: ${response.status}`);
    }
    return response.json();
  })
  .then(data => console.log(data))
  .catch(error => console.error(error));
```

Throwing inside `.then` causes the chain to jump straight to `.catch`.

lessons.starter:
fetch("https://api.example.com/items")
  .then(response => {
    // add the guard here
    return response.json();
  })
  .then(data => console.log(data))
  .catch(err => console.error(err));

lessons.solution:
