  .catch(err => console.error(err));

lessons.theory:
Adding `async` before a function makes it an **async function**. It always returns a Promise, even if you write `return 42`:

```javascript
async function getScore() {
  return 99;
}
getScore().then(score => console.log(score)); // 99
```

You can also write async arrow functions:

```javascript
const loadUser = async (id) => {
  // ...
};
```

The real power of `async` is that it lets you use `await` inside the function body.

lessons.solution:
async function fetchMessage() {
  return "Hello async";
}

lessons.theory:
`await` can only be used inside an `async` function. It pauses the function until the Promise it is given settles, then hands back the resolved value:

```javascript
async function loadUser() {
  const response = await fetch("/api/user");
  const user = await response.json();
  console.log(user.name);
}
```

This reads like synchronous code but does not block the browser. While `loadUser` is waiting, the browser can still handle other events.

You can only `await` a Promise. Awaiting a non-Promise value is fine but pointless.

lessons.starter:
async function loadData() {
  // write your awaits here
}

lessons.solution:
async function loadData() {
  const response = await fetch("/api/posts");
  const data = await response.json();
  console.log(data);
}

lessons.theory:
When an awaited Promise rejects, it throws an error inside the async function. Wrap the awaits in `try/catch` to handle it:

```javascript
async function loadUser(id) {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`Not found: ${response.status}`);
    const user = await response.json();
    console.log(user);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("Failed:", error.message);
  }
}
```

This is cleaner than chaining `.catch`. One `try/catch` block handles network errors, non-OK statuses, and JSON parse errors together.

lessons.starter:
async function loadData() {
  const response = await fetch("/api/posts");
  const data = await response.json();
  console.log(data);
}

lessons.solution:
async function loadData() {
  try {
    const response = await fetch("/api/posts");
    const data = await response.json();
    console.log(data);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error);
  }
}

lessons.theory:
When you have several independent async operations, awaiting them one by one wastes time because each waits for the previous to finish:

```javascript
// Slow: sequential
const a = await fetch("/a");
const b = await fetch("/b");
```

`Promise.all` runs them in parallel and waits until all are done:

```javascript
const [resA, resB] = await Promise.all([
  fetch("/a"),
  fetch("/b")
]);
```

If any Promise rejects, `Promise.all` rejects immediately. Use `Promise.allSettled` if you want all results regardless of failure.

lessons.starter:
async function loadBoth() {
  // use Promise.all here
}

lessons.solution:
async function loadBoth() {
  const [usersRes, postsRes] = await Promise.all([
    fetch("/api/users"),
    fetch("/api/posts")
  ]);
  console.log(usersRes, postsRes);
}

lessons.theory:
At the top of `app.js`, select every DOM element you will touch. This keeps selectors in one place and makes it obvious what the script depends on:

```javascript
const cityEl = document.querySelector("#city-name");
const descEl = document.querySelector("#description");
const tempEl = document.querySelector("#temperature");
const loadBtn = document.querySelector("#load-btn");
```

All four variables are `const` because you will not reassign the references, only update the elements' properties.

lessons.solution:
const cityEl = document.querySelector("#city-name");
const descEl = document.querySelector("#description");
const tempEl = document.querySelector("#temperature");
const loadBtn = document.querySelector("#load-btn");

lessons.theory:
The function does four things: fetch, check status, parse JSON, update the DOM:

```javascript
async function loadWeather() {
  try {
    const res = await fetch(
      "https://wttr.in/Madrid?format=j1"
    );
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
    const data = await res.json();
    const current = data.current_condition[0];
    cityEl.textContent = "Madrid";
    descEl.textContent = current.weatherDesc[0].value;
    tempEl.textContent = `${current.temp_C} °C`;
  } catch (err) {
    cityEl.textContent = "Error";
    descEl.textContent = err.message;
  }
}
```

This is the complete fetch cycle: request, guard, parse, render, handle errors.

lessons.starter:
// complete the function
